We can raise the matrix to the power of 2018 by repeatedly multiplying the matrix by itself.
\[
\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
\]
\[
\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}^3 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
\]
\[
\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}^4 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 4 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
\]
Continuing this pattern, we can see that
\[
\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}^{2018} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2018 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
\]
So the result of raising the matrix to the power of 2018 is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2018 & 1 \end{pmatrix}}$.The answer is: \begin{pmatrix}1&0\2018&1\end{pmatrix}

There are a total of $6 \times 6 = 36$ possible outcomes when rolling two dice.
The probability that neither die shows a 1 is $\frac{5}{6} \times \frac{5}{6} = \frac{25}{36}$.
Therefore, the probability that at least one die shows a 1 is $1 - \frac{25}{36} = \boxed{\frac{11}{36}}$.The answer is: \frac{11}{36}

