solution_steps:
We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (GCD).
First, we find the GCD of 128 and 144:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(128, 144) &= \text{GCD}(144, 128 - 144) \\
&= \text{GCD}(144, -16).
\end{align*}
Since the GCD should be positive, we take the absolute value:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(144, -16) &= \text{GCD}(144, 16).
\end{align*}
Now, we find the GCD of 144 and 16:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(144, 16) &= \text{GCD}(16, 144 - 9 \cdot 16) \\
&= \text{GCD}(16, 0).
\end{align*}
Therefore, the GCD of 128 and 144 is 16.
Next, we find the GCD of 16 and 480:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(16, 480) &= \text{GCD}(480, 16 \cdot 30) \\
&= \text{GCD}(480, 480) \\
&= 480.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 128, 144, and 480 is $\boxed{16}$.The answer is: 16

