A dilation centered at the origin with scale factor $-3$ will stretch or compress vectors away from or towards the origin by a factor of $-3$.
In matrix form, this dilation can be represented as
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 & 0 \\
0 & -3 \\
\end{pmatrix}
\]
This matrix will multiply the coordinates of a vector to produce the coordinates of the dilated vector.
For example, if we have a vector $\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}$, the dilated vector will be
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 & 0 \\
0 & -3 \\
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-3x \\
-3y \\
\end{pmatrix}
\]
So, the matrix that corresponds to a dilation centered at the origin with scale factor $-3$ is
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 & 0 \\
0 & -3 \\
\end{pmatrix}
}
\]The answer is: \begin{pmatrix}-3&0\0&-3\end{pmatrix}

