label("$\mathbf{v}$", V, N);
label("$5 \mathbf{v}$", 5*V, N);
label("$\mathbf{w}$", W, N);
label("$\mathbf{p}$", P, N);
label("$\mathbf{p} - 5 \mathbf{v}$", P - 5*V, N);
[/asy]

Hence,
\[\mathbf{w} \cdot (\mathbf{p} - 5 \mathbf{v}) = 0.\]Then $\mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{p} = 5 \mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{v}.$

But $\mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{p} = \|\mathbf{p}\| \|\mathbf{w}\| \cos \theta,$ where $\theta$ is the angle between $\mathbf{p}$ and $\mathbf{w}.$  Also,
\[\mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{v} = \|\mathbf{w}\| \|\mathbf{v}\| \cos \theta.\]Hence,
\[\frac{\|\mathbf{p}\|}{\|\mathbf{v}\|} = 5.\]Since $\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix},$
\[\|\mathbf{v}\| = \sqrt{3^2 + 2^2} = \sqrt{13}.\]Then $\|\mathbf{p}\| = 5 \sqrt{13},$ so
\[\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} (5 \mathbf{v}) = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 15 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}}.\]The answer is: \begin{pmatrix}15\10\end{pmatrix}

